+## Dependency Reuse
+
+A dependency in a root manifest or workspace catalog is not automatically available to every package. For Directive #16, confirm the owning package directly declares or inherits it and that the resolved version exposes the proposed API.
+
+Do not flag:
+
+- A workspace dependency that the owning package does not inherit
+- An API behind a feature that the owning package does not enable
+- A helper that deliberately changes formatting, error behavior, ownership, side effects, or protocol semantics
+- A local facade or newtype that protects application invariants or a stable public boundary
+- A small implementation when the proposed replacement requires a new dependency, feature, or unrelated migration
+- Code based only on current upstream documentation when the project resolves an older API
+
+A library can expose a similar name without providing equivalent behavior. Check exact call sites and tests. For example, a checksum address formatter is not a replacement for a lowercase, unprefixed database key.
+
+If an exact replacement exists, retain any local facade that is part of the application's public contract and replace only its duplicated internals. Remove a now-unused dependency only after checking all targets and feature-specific code.
+
